<%# THE RUNWAY — this period as a line, with a mark on every day money is needed (two-shapes spec §3).
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    ** IT IS THE PERIOD BAR'S SUCCESSOR AND IT ANSWERS A QUESTION THE BAR COULD NOT. ** The hero drew
    "day 7 of 14" and nothing else; the four dated rules falling due inside those fourteen days were
    scattered down the section below as clauses, so "is there anything coming?" took a scroll and a
    subtraction. Here the period is a ruler, today is a point on it, and every day money has to BE
    there is a point further along.

    ** ONE TICK PER DATED RULE DUE INSIDE THIS PERIOD, AND THE PERIOD IS `User#period_containing`. **
    No date arithmetic lives in this file: every position is `HomePresenter::Runway`'s own 1-based
    day over the same `Progress` the marker is drawn from, so a tick cannot be placed on a period the
    marker is not on. Absent entirely for a user who has declared no period — the refusal
    `#period_progress` has always made, because a runway is a picture OF a period and a calendar
    month nobody set would be thirty invented days.

    ** THE THREE COLOURS. ** Green is ready (the money is there), red is short (it is not, and the
    day is inside this period), and the olive — `--color-primary-dark`, the accruing colour — is the
    RAIL: the part of the period that has not happened yet. A tick is never olive, and that is this
    task's ruling: a rule due before the next period either has its money or does not, so "still
    accruing" is a sentence about a rule due LATER, which is where `HomeHelper#when_words` says it.

    ** THE MARKS AND THE WORDS ARE STACKED RATHER THAN OVERLAID. ** The mock labels each mark in
    place; two bills three days apart on a 375px screen put their labels through each other, and a
    label that has to be read is worth more than one that has to be positioned. The rail carries the
    marks (with the full label in a `title`), and the list under it carries the words in date order —
    the same ticks, twice, one for the shape and one for the reading. %>
<% if runway = presenter.runway %>
  <section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-4 sm:p-6" data-runway aria-labelledby="runway-heading">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
      <h3 id="runway-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">This period</h3>
      <%# THE SAME SENTENCE THE HERO'S BAR CARRIED, and it is now printed once on this screen: the
          section below reads its heading off the blocks, not off the calendar. %>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500" data-period-days-left><%= pluralize(runway.days_left, "day") %> left</p>
    </div>

    <%# THE RAIL. The elapsed part is filled and the rest is the accruing olive; `role="progressbar"`
        because a bar with no accessible value is decoration to a screen reader, and the days-left
        sentence beside it is the value in words either way. The percent rides on the TRACK for
        `_this_period`'s reason — a 0% fill has no width and cannot be found. %>
    <div class="relative mt-4 h-2 rounded-full bg-brand-dark"
         role="progressbar"
         aria-valuenow="<%= runway.percent %>"
         aria-valuemin="0"
         aria-valuemax="100"
         aria-label="Day <%= runway.day %> of <%= runway.days %> in this period"
         data-period-progress="<%= runway.percent %>">
      <div class="absolute inset-y-0 left-0 rounded-full bg-gray-300" style="width: <%= runway.percent %>%;"></div>

      <%# TODAY, as a mark on the ruler rather than as the end of a fill: what is left of the period
          is the thing the ticks are placed in, so the day itself has to be findable. %>
      <span class="absolute top-1/2 h-4 w-0.5 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded bg-gray-900"
            style="left: <%= runway.percent %>%;"
            title="Today"
            data-today-mark></span>

      <% runway.ticks.each do |tick| %>
        <span class="absolute top-1/2 h-3 w-3 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full border border-white <%= tick.short? ? "bg-status-danger" : "bg-status-success" %>"
              style="left: <%= tick.percent %>%;"
              title="<%= number_to_currency(tick.amount) %> <%= tick.label %>"
              data-tick-mark="<%= tick.label %>"></span>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <%# THE PERIOD'S OWN ENDS, labelled: the ruler is meaningless without them, and "Sep 1 — Sep 30"
        is where every tick's date gets its scale. %>
    <div class="mt-1.5 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 text-xs text-gray-500" data-period-range>
      <span><%= runway.first.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
      <span><%= runway.last.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
    </div>

    <%# THE TICKS AS WORDS, in the order the rail puts them.
        AT 375 EACH TICK IS ITS OWN LINE (`flex-col`), amount then name, so two bills three days
        apart never share a row and nothing has to be read sideways; from 640 they sit side by side
        with the amount ABOVE the name, which is the mock's two-line label with room for it. The
        state is said in words as well as in colour, because a colour is not a sentence and a red dot
        with no word beside it is a screen asking to be interpreted. %>
    <% if runway.any_due? %>
      <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-2 sm:flex-row sm:flex-wrap sm:gap-4">
        <% runway.ticks.each do |tick| %>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 text-sm sm:flex-col sm:items-start sm:gap-0"
              data-tick="<%= tick.label %>">
            <span class="font-medium tabular-nums <%= tick.short? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
              <%= number_to_currency(tick.amount) %>
            </span>
            <%# NAME · DAY · STATE, which is the order the block row's own clause puts them in
                (`HomeHelper#when_words`): what it is, when it is, and whether the money is there. %>
            <span class="text-xs text-gray-600">
              <%= tick.label %> ·
              <span class="tabular-nums"><%= tick.line.next_due_on.strftime("%b %-d") %></span> ·
              <%= tick.short? ? "#{number_to_currency(tick.gap)} short" : "ready" %>
            </span>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE PACE LINE (§3), which is three sentences and never more:

          what a day may cost from here      — both signs of `free`, `HomeHelper#pace_words`
          what falls due before the period closes — Σ the ticks, absent when nothing does
          which of them is short, by name    — absent when none is

        THE SECOND AND THIRD ARE ABSENT RATHER THAN ZEROED. "$0.00 due before Sep 30" is a line that
        reports nothing, and a period with nothing due is the ordinary one. %>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-gray-100 pt-3 space-y-1" data-pace>
      <% if pace = presenter.pace_line %>
        <p class="text-sm <%= pace.fine? ? "text-gray-700" : "text-status-danger" %>" data-pace-line>
          <%= pace_words(pace) %>
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <% if runway.any_due? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 tabular-nums" data-due-total>
          <%= number_to_currency(runway.due_total) %> due before <%= runway.last.strftime("%b %-d") %>.
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <%# NAMED, ONE BY ONE, because "2 rules are short" is a fact the user cannot act on. The gap is
          `ClaimLine#fund_gap` — the same figure the trouble strip prints about the same rule. %>
      <% if runway.short.any? %>
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-status-danger" data-short-list>
          <%= safe_join(runway.short.map { |tick| "#{tick.label} is #{number_to_currency(tick.gap)} short" }, ", ") %>.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>
<% end %>
